i  very much enjoyed this i thought it was  quite a wild story  when the opportunity to play one of  these nine perfect strangers came your  way what was it that got you  most interested and excited about it  melissa mccarthy she's the one that  reached out and uh let me know about it  and  she's she's a good friend and she was  looking out for me and she said you know  this could be interesting because we've  never done this before i've never seen  you play this kind of character he's  really interesting  so i read it and uh and i read the book  and and i thought um  i just thought it was a good exploration  of trauma and  the different ways that people try to  work through that  and grief and uh and i thought hey in  particular the character was interesting  because he was  like a dichotomy you know he was a guy  who volunteered to be there but then  spent all of his time really  doing everything he he could to get out  of there  and i thought that was a real challenge  to figure out how to make that  interesting and  and i thought it was pretty funny too  you know that somebody could be that  in the depths that deep and yet i  thought there could be some comedic  gold to mine there is there ever a worry  when a friend like that sends you a  project that like what  what do you do if you don't like it are  you someone who is pretty honest like  would you say you know i just i don't  think this works for me  yeah i mean you have to be honest and i  have been in situations like that where  i've had friends um luckily i haven't  had any friendships break up over my  not wanting to do it but um you do have  to be honest you know it's a long time  to spend with  with um a project you know like you  would hope that everybody that's  involved is there because they want to  be there and they believe in it so i  it'd be a tough thing for me to uh  to fake my way through whose decision  was the beard did you comp  you know contemplate whether to have a  beard or not to have a beard or were you  always going to have a beard for him  no that was me that was my i wanted to  grow the beard i wanted to wear that hat  nobody wanted me to wear that hat that  hat was a big fight but i just thought  i just thought he was a guy who really  didn't want to you know really didn't  want to be recognized  it has probably happened to him before  he does get recognized it happens in the  script and i just thought  what would this guy try to do to really  really be unrecognizable and so i  i thought well he'd he's addicted to  painkillers so he definitely would gain  weight so i did that and then um  and then yeah i grew the beard um  yeah really just to lend lend a little  bit more mystery to the guy a little  more  kind of letting himself go you know i  think the guy's at the end of his rope  when we meet him and so  i think he's trying his best to  disappear and uh i wanted him to look  that way it's why i wanted his clothes  to be  20 years old if you're gonna  get screwed around with by anyone it  seems like nicole kidman  is a good choice to have do that to you  what did you most enjoy about exploring  the dynamic with her  character and how is the experience of  having her as a scene partner  in this role yeah well so you know the  character  i like very much how the character  brings an air of mystery  about them um and yet the actor who  plays them has to be filled with  a really deep well of complexity um and  you know nicole's been doing this for a  while she's played a lot of different  roles and i i'm always impressed with  just how  much she takes on you know it's it's  hard to work that much and  not repeat yourself and and she's a  great example of somebody who just  challenges herself so much so  i was excited to get to work with her  because i thought well this is such a  weird character there's just so much  going on here but  you know the writing supports that  history and particularly the way that  that information is parsed out in the  show and how we figure out how we  learn about her experience and her  trauma i thought that the character was  compelling on the page  um and i just knew that she was going to  bring something to it that  would make those scenes charge  particularly those early up those  early scenes with tony when we don't  know enough about we don't know  a lot about either of them but something  happens  in that room i think tony's the kind of  character that like  you you you kind of he's so forceful  that character his nature is so forceful  that it's a good idea to have him in  conflict with her  very early on when you don't know much  about them and  and so it was uh i looked forward to  shooting those scenes and we did shoot  them pretty early when  i didn't really know nicole that much so  there's this sort of sense of trying to  figure each other out in that scene that  was very real  there's also some wild stuff that  happens in the show could you ever  have imagined that you do a project  where you had a scene  in which luke evans was birthing your  baby i mean what was that  like to shoot funny i've  almost forgot about that scene i don't  know how but maybe it's a mental block  oh we laughed so hard we laughed so hard  and then you've got everybody in the  cast  at the foot of the bed yeah you know  loving it  it was so weird it was so weird but you  know  it's a dream sequence so that's what's  fun about a dream sequence you can do  anything i think there's another scene  if it's still in there where i've got  like a long haired wig um  and uh like i'm like a romance novelist  cover boy  um and those are always fun because you  can you can do anything in a dream right  uh and when you when you  when you consider why they're having  those kinds of dreams  it's even more fascinating i think but  that's  like a reveal that comes later in the  show what is your reaction to the ending  of the series and where things are left  for all of these characters but  specifically for your character  well you know i think it's um i like the  ending of it you know  um i think some things are left unsaid  some things are left pretty vague i  think that like the  characters heal in some way or another  they're not completely healed but  they're not completely broken i think at  the beginning of the  of the story they're all pretty close to  completely broken whether they're  whether they're showing it or not but  all of those characters at one point  luke's character says you know she chose  us specifically she curated us and i  think that that's true and i think the  thing they have in common is that they  are  as broken as one can possibly be and so  by the end of that thing  the end of that experience um i think  that they're in a better place certainly  than they were when they got there and i  think  you know if there's any message to be  taken away there might be something to  taking the time for yourself to um shake  it up a bit shake the tree as it were  was there a most challenging part of  this shoot or most challenging scene or  is it all just  fun when you're doing something as kind  of wild and crazy as this gets  you know i think the physical thing was  was the like  the more physical scenes were  challenging um if only because i just i  gained so much weight and i wasn't  really  taking good care of myself and um and so  anytime i had to run i was i was a bit  worried because  i've got bad knees and i wasn't in shape  and  and i had to yet i had to cut and i was  playing this injury you know and  so for me it was more physically  challenging than other roles but  you know a big part of that was just  really having to continually stay in a  place of  physical pain you know that's something  that jonathan levine and i talked a lot  about that this guy doesn't like get up  from a chair without it really  hurting and so sort of living in that  place was  something that i had to stay very  focused on and  and that was that was that was pretty  challenging well i appreciate you  talking to me about it i found all these  characters so  interesting so thank you oh thanks  christina thank you  thanks a lot

